(348774, 26239, 6155, '<td valign="top">the picture makes the sheep look friendlier than it did in the flesh<br/>actually looks cute and cuddly here</td>', 'the picture makes the sheep look friendlier than it did in the fleshactually looks cute and cuddly here', '2003-07-07 06:24:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(348958, 26074, 9854, '<td valign="top">Nice elements. I would have liked seeing a more focused patch of dandelions, though... or perhaps lessen the amount of unfocused space that hogs the middle part.</td>', 'Nice elements. I would have liked seeing a more focused patch of dandelions, though... or perhaps lessen the amount of unfocused space that hogs the middle part.', '2003-07-07 11:56:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(348973, 26234, 3087, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts</b> <br/>A bit pink and tilted to right.<br/> <br/><b>Content</b> <br/>The content itself is sweet though not of huge interest to me. It conveys Country Life to a certain extent, though I think it conveys a certain style of architecture even more than that, (though I appreciate it may be a style more prevalent in the country_.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>I like the framing on the left from the tree and flower bed foliage. <br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Light seems a bit harsh at this time of day  I think it would be worth looking at shooting at different time of day and using a fill in flash to throw light into the porchway.<br/> <br/><b>Processing</b> <br/>Needs rotation. When deciding how much to rotate I usually focus on the aspect of the picture that seems to be the main focal point  in this case that seems to be the door itself. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo</b> <br/>An acceptable (and nicely composed) photo of a pretty home but not an image that pulls me in in terms of content or capture.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club  Initial thoughts A bit pink and tilted to right. Content The content itself is sweet though not of huge interest to me. It conveys Country Life to a certain extent, though I think it conveys a certain style of architecture even more than that, (though I appreciate it may be a style more prevalent in the country_. Composition I like the framing on the left from the tree and flower bed foliage.  Technical Light seems a bit harsh at this time of day  I think it would be worth looking at shooting at different time of day and using a fill in flash to throw light into the porchway. Processing Needs rotation. When deciding how much to rotate I usually focus on the aspect of the picture that seems to be the main focal point  in this case that seems to be the door itself.  My Opinion On The Photo An acceptable (and nicely composed) photo of a pretty home but not an image that pulls me in in terms of content or capture.', '2003-07-07 12:19:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(348980, 26238, 6202, '<td valign="top">Neat shot Kavey.  The steam creates some really strong leading lines but there is a small problem with those line in this photo, just as it makes the turn it makes you feel like you are missing something on that point of the stream and then as you follow it further you see the cows but it keeps going and when I look at it I feel like there should be a cow at the very end of the line as you follow it.  But I know you can''t do anything about that<br/> <br/>Now that I am done telling you what I don''t like about it I think overall it has a very good dof used the lighting as natural as it is, works very well, I''m guessing you took it on an overcast day as there is no shadowing cast by the cows.  <br/> <br/>From one dimage 7i user to another, did you shoot it using manual focus set on infinity?  I''m asking this cause everything seems to be in relatively good focus. <br/> <br/>As I read the comments left behind I can''t help but notice everyone says about the cows being to far away, truthfully they aren''t that far away, but the title makes you think they are your main subject...without the title you don''t notice that they are so far away.  It is a really nice pasture photo and does meet the country challenge.  <br/> <br/>Anna</td>', 'Neat shot Kavey.  The steam creates some really strong leading lines but there is a small problem with those line in this photo, just as it makes the turn it makes you feel like you are missing something on that point of the stream and then as you follow it further you see the cows but it keeps going and when I look at it I feel like there should be a cow at the very end of the line as you follow it.  But I know you can''t do anything about that Now that I am done telling you what I don''t like about it I think overall it has a very good dof used the lighting as natural as it is, works very well, I''m guessing you took it on an overcast day as there is no shadowing cast by the cows.   From one dimage 7i user to another, did you shoot it using manual focus set on infinity?  I''m asking this cause everything seems to be in relatively good focus.  As I read the comments left behind I can''t help but notice everyone says about the cows being to far away, truthfully they aren''t that far away, but the title makes you think they are your main subject...without the title you don''t notice that they are so far away.  It is a really nice pasture photo and does meet the country challenge.   Anna', '2003-07-07 12:24:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(348984, 26238, 714, '<td valign="top">Kavey, I hope you don''t mind me giving you some tips on making this photo better.  <br/>Try moving over a bit to the left so you can get the whole creek.  I think that might get you closer to the cows.  Or maybe just cropping off the bottom part of the picture and zoom in on the cows more.  I think the time of day was really bad here.  The sky, water and cows seem to be washed out.  Try early morning or late afternoon.  Sepia would have worked too for this shot.</td>', 'Kavey, I hope you don''t mind me giving you some tips on making this photo better.  Try moving over a bit to the left so you can get the whole creek.  I think that might get you closer to the cows.  Or maybe just cropping off the bottom part of the picture and zoom in on the cows more.  I think the time of day was really bad here.  The sky, water and cows seem to be washed out.  Try early morning or late afternoon.  Sepia would have worked too for this shot.', '2003-07-07 12:29:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(349016, 26238, 3087, '<td valign="top">Thanks for both your comments!<br/> <br/>The reason it looks as though something is missing at what likes an elbow bend is because it was actually a junction which went three ways! I tried moving around a little to see if I could work a composition which included it but it didn''t balance well.<br/> <br/>I absolutely agree about the somewhat washed out colours, and the comment about the overcase weather is a good point - the sun wasn''t shining at that moment, as it had been for most of the trip - I guess it was such a relief on the temperature front I didn''t stop to think about the impact on the colour and lights in my picture.<br/> <br/>I took it on autofocus, focusing on the cows, mainly because that usually gives a good range of focus when not too close to the object and also because I literally leapt out of the car and took a few shots really fast - the car was blocking the road completely and, although no one was coming, we wanted to get off the narrow road (with water on either side) before we met someone coming the opposite direction!<br/> <br/>I also agree, I''d like the cows to be a little closer, preferably in the centre of that triangle area.<br/> <br/>I really appreciate the comments, thanks to both of you.<br/></td>', 'Thanks for both your comments! The reason it looks as though something is missing at what likes an elbow bend is because it was actually a junction which went three ways! I tried moving around a little to see if I could work a composition which included it but it didn''t balance well. I absolutely agree about the somewhat washed out colours, and the comment about the overcase weather is a good point - the sun wasn''t shining at that moment, as it had been for most of the trip - I guess it was such a relief on the temperature front I didn''t stop to think about the impact on the colour and lights in my picture. I took it on autofocus, focusing on the cows, mainly because that usually gives a good range of focus when not too close to the object and also because I literally leapt out of the car and took a few shots really fast - the car was blocking the road completely and, although no one was coming, we wanted to get off the narrow road (with water on either side) before we met someone coming the opposite direction! I also agree, I''d like the cows to be a little closer, preferably in the centre of that triangle area. I really appreciate the comments, thanks to both of you.', '2003-07-07 12:57:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(349125, 26098, 472, '<td valign="top">this picture is really amazing.  it has grown and grown on me all week as it sits on the front page.  good job</td>', 'this picture is really amazing.  it has grown and grown on me all week as it sits on the front page.  good job', '2003-07-07 14:42:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(349232, 26258, 4285, '<td valign="top">This was one of my highest votes cast in this challenge. I thought it had country written all over it. Maybe if you had sepia toned it; that seems to be what makes winners here of late. I''m kidding of course about the sepia. It''s a beautiful shot as is.<br/> <br/>Owen</td>', 'This was one of my highest votes cast in this challenge. I thought it had country written all over it. Maybe if you had sepia toned it; that seems to be what makes winners here of late. I''m kidding of course about the sepia. It''s a beautiful shot as is. Owen', '2003-07-07 16:08:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(349649, 25801, 6900, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the WELL deserved ribbon.</td>', 'Congrats on the WELL deserved ribbon.', '2003-07-07 22:35:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(349739, 25801, 714, '<td valign="top">Wow!  I didn''t realize this was 3rd. Where have I been? <br/> <br/>Great photo and a big congrats, David!!!!</td>', 'Wow!  I didn''t realize this was 3rd. Where have I been?  Great photo and a big congrats, David!!!!', '2003-07-08 00:08:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(349765, 25532, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>Because the pineapple is the symbol for hospitality, and because you often see replication of them around plantations/farm houses, etc., here in the Southern US, I can see your link to the challenge, albeit a strenuous one. :-)<br/> <br/>The composition of your photo looks balanced and well framed.  The two forms of the pineapple help to balance each side and give the eyes somewhere to rest, while still providing "movement" in the image.  Also, I like the contrasting angles of what I assume is "greenery" in the image.<br/> <br/>What I cannot comment on are the technical aspects of the image, such as focus, lighting, etc.  Though this is a valid effect to use, it does tend to make it more of a digital image than a photograph, which does not typically do well on here.  I, for one, find it interesting, but can''t help but wonder if you were trying to disguise a really, really, really bad picture.  I looked at your profile, and I just assume you like this style.  That''s fine.<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat Because the pineapple is the symbol for hospitality, and because you often see replication of them around plantations/farm houses, etc., here in the Southern US, I can see your link to the challenge, albeit a strenuous one. :-) The composition of your photo looks balanced and well framed.  The two forms of the pineapple help to balance each side and give the eyes somewhere to rest, while still providing "movement" in the image.  Also, I like the contrasting angles of what I assume is "greenery" in the image. What I cannot comment on are the technical aspects of the image, such as focus, lighting, etc.  Though this is a valid effect to use, it does tend to make it more of a digital image than a photograph, which does not typically do well on here.  I, for one, find it interesting, but can''t help but wonder if you were trying to disguise a really, really, really bad picture.  I looked at your profile, and I just assume you like this style.  That''s fine. Best to you in future challenges. karmat', '2003-07-08 00:42:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(349769, 26065, 7711, '<td valign="top">Hi Peggy!<br/> <br/><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>I didn''t get a chance to vote on this picture during the challenge, but I hope I can offer you some comments to help you along in your photography. <br/> <br/><b>Colour, Composition, Contrast:</b> <br/> <br/>You got fewer comments on this photography than many pictures get, but you were lucky in that all of your commenters offered you some constructive comments. <br/> <br/>The elements I notice in your shot are the horses and the tree in the foreground. I like that you chose to frame your shot using the trees, it adds to the country feel, but because they appear to be in shadow, their colours are more saturated and more in focus than the rest of the shot. This takes away from the horses, who are already suffering from a lack of sharpness in their focus. Indeed they appear very small in the shot, and I would have liked it if you included less tree and got a bit closer to the animals. <br/> <br/><b>Focus and Lighting</b> <br/> <br/>I agree that the colours are being drowned out by the light. Did you consider trying this shot again when the sun was lower in the sky? Perhaps nearer to sunset? (Or even just after sunrise if you are an early riser!)<br/> <br/>The effect of the light is to wash out the colours of the grass and the horses, which doesn''t hold our attention as much as it could. The colours are washed out, and a lower, softer light would have really made this shot pop.<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/> <br/>I liked the idea here, and your composition. The colours are not true to life, the horses are far off and the lighting is flat, all of which are easily correctable technical problems which would made this shot for you. :) Happy shooting!</td>', 'Hi Peggy! Greetings from the Critique Club  I didn''t get a chance to vote on this picture during the challenge, but I hope I can offer you some comments to help you along in your photography.  Colour, Composition, Contrast:  You got fewer comments on this photography than many pictures get, but you were lucky in that all of your commenters offered you some constructive comments.  The elements I notice in your shot are the horses and the tree in the foreground. I like that you chose to frame your shot using the trees, it adds to the country feel, but because they appear to be in shadow, their colours are more saturated and more in focus than the rest of the shot. This takes away from the horses, who are already suffering from a lack of sharpness in their focus. Indeed they appear very small in the shot, and I would have liked it if you included less tree and got a bit closer to the animals.  Focus and Lighting  I agree that the colours are being drowned out by the light. Did you consider trying this shot again when the sun was lower in the sky? Perhaps nearer to sunset? (Or even just after sunrise if you are an early riser!) The effect of the light is to wash out the colours of the grass and the horses, which doesn''t hold our attention as much as it could. The colours are washed out, and a lower, softer light would have really made this shot pop.   Overall  I liked the idea here, and your composition. The colours are not true to life, the horses are far off and the lighting is flat, all of which are easily correctable technical problems which would made this shot for you. :) Happy shooting!', '2003-07-08 00:48:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(349773, 26265, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>You have met the challenge very well.<br/> <br/>I really like the way you have chosen to compose this shot.  The horses provide a nice focal point for the eyes to "rest" on, and the "negative space" (for lack of a better term), forces the eyes to go to the left side of the frame.  I also like that one horse is further back which helps to draw the eyes "into" the frame.<br/> <br/>The focus is great, and it allows the detail of the horses and surrounding countryside to show up.  Part of me doesn''t like the coloring, maybe a bit too red/orange/brown, but it does help to show the dramatic clouds in the sky. <br/> <br/>Great work, this is an awesome shot.<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat You have met the challenge very well. I really like the way you have chosen to compose this shot.  The horses provide a nice focal point for the eyes to "rest" on, and the "negative space" (for lack of a better term), forces the eyes to go to the left side of the frame.  I also like that one horse is further back which helps to draw the eyes "into" the frame. The focus is great, and it allows the detail of the horses and surrounding countryside to show up.  Part of me doesn''t like the coloring, maybe a bit too red/orange/brown, but it does help to show the dramatic clouds in the sky.  Great work, this is an awesome shot. Best to you in future challenges.karmat', '2003-07-08 00:56:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(349785, 26011, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>I find this very stunning, I really liked it during the voting and I still do =o)<br/> <br/>I find the use of color here to make this entire photo.  Pulling out the oranges makes this photo standout from the others.  I am not about to sit here and try to pick this apart when I like it so much.  Only question I have is why did you put the border on it?  The photo is so pretty without it and I''m not sure the border did anything for it.<br/> <br/> <br/>This definately meets the challenge and was very well done.  <br/> <br/>Anna<br/></td>', '*Critique Club* I find this very stunning, I really liked it during the voting and I still do =o) I find the use of color here to make this entire photo.  Pulling out the oranges makes this photo standout from the others.  I am not about to sit here and try to pick this apart when I like it so much.  Only question I have is why did you put the border on it?  The photo is so pretty without it and I''m not sure the border did anything for it.  This definately meets the challenge and was very well done.   Anna', '2003-07-08 01:16:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(349786, 26212, 3669, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings From the Critique Club:</b> <br/> <br/>Hi, Tarbini<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b>  I do believe that this is a pretty picture that nicely shows the countryside.  Therefore, you have met the challenge well.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b>  There is absolutely nothing that could be done differently with the composition here.  The single hay bale in the front is nice.  The shadow of what must be a tree to the right provides a nice contrast and adds to the picture.  The sky is a beautiful blue, however, I still agree with my comment made during the challenge that it would be even better if you had some wispy clouds the day you took this picture.  The rolling hills provide a nice backdrop to your picture.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>  Once again, I still agree with my comment made before.  I think the picture might be just a tad on the bright side.  I would like to see the colors "pop" out just a little bit more.  The yellows and greens seem just a bit flat.  Other than that, I would say that it was perfect.  I''m not familar with circular polarizers, so I''m curious as to how it affected the scene.<br/> <br/>It was also interesting to see the comments that you recieved concerning your border.  As always, as border that differs from the plain black becomes a controversy.  I happen to like the transparent border and disagree with those that said it distracts.<br/> <br/><b>Overall:</b>  A very nice countryside picture.  I stand true to the score that I gave it during the challenge.  While it didn''t bring out the "WOW" factor, it was nicely done.  I looked at some of your other photos and am very impressed with your work.  Look forward to seeing more.<br/> <br/>Connie</td>', 'Greetings From the Critique Club:  Hi, Tarbini Challenge:  I do believe that this is a pretty picture that nicely shows the countryside.  Therefore, you have met the challenge well. Composition:  There is absolutely nothing that could be done differently with the composition here.  The single hay bale in the front is nice.  The shadow of what must be a tree to the right provides a nice contrast and adds to the picture.  The sky is a beautiful blue, however, I still agree with my comment made during the challenge that it would be even better if you had some wispy clouds the day you took this picture.  The rolling hills provide a nice backdrop to your picture. Technical:  Once again, I still agree with my comment made before.  I think the picture might be just a tad on the bright side.  I would like to see the colors "pop" out just a little bit more.  The yellows and greens seem just a bit flat.  Other than that, I would say that it was perfect.  I''m not familar with circular polarizers, so I''m curious as to how it affected the scene. It was also interesting to see the comments that you recieved concerning your border.  As always, as border that differs from the plain black becomes a controversy.  I happen to like the transparent border and disagree with those that said it distracts. Overall:  A very nice countryside picture.  I stand true to the score that I gave it during the challenge.  While it didn''t bring out the "WOW" factor, it was nicely done.  I looked at some of your other photos and am very impressed with your work.  Look forward to seeing more. Connie', '2003-07-08 01:16:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(349802, 25648, 3669, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings From the Critique Club:</b> <br/> <br/>Hi, Van<br/>You recieved many comments during the voting phase of the challenge.  You are very lucky.  I won''t be able to add much to what has already been said, but i will try.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b>  This definately is a nice picture depicting the country, therefore, meets the challenge dead on.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b>  The composition couldn''t be anymore perfect.  The windmill in the foreground with the background of the brilliant colors of the sunset make for a very pretty picture.  This is a great capture.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b>  I think the technical aspect of this photo is its drawback.  The picture seems a bit blurred or out of focus.  It cries out for clarity.  I was looking back at some of your other pictures that you have submitted in for challenges and I notice the same problem with some of them.  Are you using a tripod.  Especially when doing close-ups or shots that are done with limited light, keeping the camera steady is very important.  No matter how steady we think we are, it''s still hard not moving the camera a little when taking the shot.  I hated slugging the tripod around and fought having to use it, but once I MADE myself to use it, I found that I was getting better shots.  <br/> <br/>You have a nice 3 meg pixal camera that should be able to give you clear shots.  I was thinking that maybe another reason your shot turned out fuzzy was that you were using an advanced zoom function on your camera.  I always have problems getting scenic pictures to turn out using my advance zoom.  I found that I came out with better results if I used the highest resolution and took a picture without the zoom and then just cropped it.<br/> <br/>One more thing, someone suggested in the comments that you might put your picture through Neat Image.  Yes, you do have some grain in your photo and using Neat Image will help clear this up and give you a smoother picture, however, it will cause some loss of detail.  So, if you decide to download it, I would use it wisely.  I learned my lesson when using it in the challenges.<br/> <br/>Connie</td>', 'Greetings From the Critique Club:  Hi, VanYou recieved many comments during the voting phase of the challenge.  You are very lucky.  I won''t be able to add much to what has already been said, but i will try. Challenge:  This definately is a nice picture depicting the country, therefore, meets the challenge dead on. Composition:  The composition couldn''t be anymore perfect.  The windmill in the foreground with the background of the brilliant colors of the sunset make for a very pretty picture.  This is a great capture. Technical:  I think the technical aspect of this photo is its drawback.  The picture seems a bit blurred or out of focus.  It cries out for clarity.  I was looking back at some of your other pictures that you have submitted in for challenges and I notice the same problem with some of them.  Are you using a tripod.  Especially when doing close-ups or shots that are done with limited light, keeping the camera steady is very important.  No matter how steady we think we are, it''s still hard not moving the camera a little when taking the shot.  I hated slugging the tripod around and fought having to use it, but once I MADE myself to use it, I found that I was getting better shots.   You have a nice 3 meg pixal camera that should be able to give you clear shots.  I was thinking that maybe another reason your shot turned out fuzzy was that you were using an advanced zoom function on your camera.  I always have problems getting scenic pictures to turn out using my advance zoom.  I found that I came out with better results if I used the highest resolution and took a picture without the zoom and then just cropped it. One more thing, someone suggested in the comments that you might put your picture through Neat Image.  Yes, you do have some grain in your photo and using Neat Image will help clear this up and give you a smoother picture, however, it will cause some loss of detail.  So, if you decide to download it, I would use it wisely.  I learned my lesson when using it in the challenges. Connie', '2003-07-08 01:49:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(349812, 25801, 1717, '<td valign="top">congratulations, david!  this is really a great shot!</td>', 'congratulations, david!  this is really a great shot!', '2003-07-08 02:08:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(349895, 25801, 6172, '<td valign="top">Looks like what people in "Hyrum, Utah" do!  Nice photo, BTW, and, I gave you a "10" during voting.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-07-08 03:30:01.</i></td>', 'Looks like what people in "Hyrum, Utah" do!  Nice photo, BTW, and, I gave you a "10" during voting.', '2003-07-08 04:28:15.000000', '2003-07-08 04:30:01.000000', 0),
(349937, 26163, 6349, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>I was a little perturbed when I received this photo from the Critique Club and found the original comment I made, which may have seemed a bit rude.  Nevertheless, I will do my best to provide some more feedback.  Also, you should know that I still gave you a 5 despite the fact that I didn''t find the color manipulation pleasing.<br/> <br/>I think the composition on this is pretty good, if a little bit heavy on the right.  This may be due more to the exposure of the lighting than to the actual composition.  The bright ground on the left contrasts a bit harshly with the dark shadows in the trees and on the tractor.  The dark lighting on the tractor gives it an odd feeling since it is presumably the subject of the shot but has less detail in it.  It looks like it was exposed more for the background than for the tractor.<br/> <br/>I really love the background, by the way.  I think the lines and textures in the run down house are very appealing and along with the tractor met the challenge very well.  The focus is also right on.  The pinkness in the building and the blueness in the trees just seemd too unnatural and not fitting editing to do for a country life scene.  I like the infrared effect in general, but think it would have worked better in black and white in this instance.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-07-08 04:53:33.</i></td>', 'Critique Club: I was a little perturbed when I received this photo from the Critique Club and found the original comment I made, which may have seemed a bit rude.  Nevertheless, I will do my best to provide some more feedback.  Also, you should know that I still gave you a 5 despite the fact that I didn''t find the color manipulation pleasing. I think the composition on this is pretty good, if a little bit heavy on the right.  This may be due more to the exposure of the lighting than to the actual composition.  The bright ground on the left contrasts a bit harshly with the dark shadows in the trees and on the tractor.  The dark lighting on the tractor gives it an odd feeling since it is presumably the subject of the shot but has less detail in it.  It looks like it was exposed more for the background than for the tractor. I really love the background, by the way.  I think the lines and textures in the run down house are very appealing and along with the tractor met the challenge very well.  The focus is also right on.  The pinkness in the building and the blueness in the trees just seemd too unnatural and not fitting editing to do for a country life scene.  I like the infrared effect in general, but think it would have worked better in black and white in this instance.', '2003-07-08 05:53:03.000000', '2003-07-08 05:53:33.000000', 0),
(349940, 25872, 6349, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Greetings!  There''s not a lot of major comments I can make about this that haven''t already been said by other people.  I agree with all of them.  This would have benefitted tremendously by being in black and white or sepia to give it an old look to match the scene and it would have been better to move in closer to make the scene less busy.  I''m guessing that you took this passing through and felt awkard asking these people if they minded posing for a picture.<br/> <br/>All in all, I thought this met the challenge very well and in an interesting way but the image itself isn''t what it could have been if you had really stuck around and worked the scene a bit.  Even as it is now, without taking another photo, I think it might benefit from a tighter cropping, if you didn''t lose resolution.<br/> <br/>The other question I found myself asking when I saw this shot is:  would they have really had a pineapple?  :)<br/></td>', 'Critique Club: Greetings!  There''s not a lot of major comments I can make about this that haven''t already been said by other people.  I agree with all of them.  This would have benefitted tremendously by being in black and white or sepia to give it an old look to match the scene and it would have been better to move in closer to make the scene less busy.  I''m guessing that you took this passing through and felt awkard asking these people if they minded posing for a picture. All in all, I thought this met the challenge very well and in an interesting way but the image itself isn''t what it could have been if you had really stuck around and worked the scene a bit.  Even as it is now, without taking another photo, I think it might benefit from a tighter cropping, if you didn''t lose resolution. The other question I found myself asking when I saw this shot is:  would they have really had a pineapple?  :)', '2003-07-08 06:01:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(349943, 25956, 6349, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Hey, funny I should get this for the critique club after already corresponding with you about it.  As you already know, I liked this shot alot.  I also think you already know what''s good and what''s bad about your photo.<br/> <br/>This shot is great in terms of tone and focus (in my opinion, obviously not everyones) but lacking in terms of composition and lighting, which were both out of your control.  I think you did a nice job of minimizing the blown out highlights of the sun behind the trees.  I didn''t find it that distracting, but it would surely be better without them.  I actually find the highlight on the horses nose (not sure what a horses nose is called :) interesting because I think it is a white marking, but I''m not sure.  I think it is an interesting, even if unintended, effect of drawing the eye there.<br/> <br/>All in all, it''s a damn shame that the horses didn''t want to cooperate and pose professionally for and that you can''t jump over fences and move other people''s horses.  I think this is a beautiful photo and you should be very proud of how it came out given the circumstances.</td>', 'Critique Club: Hey, funny I should get this for the critique club after already corresponding with you about it.  As you already know, I liked this shot alot.  I also think you already know what''s good and what''s bad about your photo. This shot is great in terms of tone and focus (in my opinion, obviously not everyones) but lacking in terms of composition and lighting, which were both out of your control.  I think you did a nice job of minimizing the blown out highlights of the sun behind the trees.  I didn''t find it that distracting, but it would surely be better without them.  I actually find the highlight on the horses nose (not sure what a horses nose is called :) interesting because I think it is a white marking, but I''m not sure.  I think it is an interesting, even if unintended, effect of drawing the eye there. All in all, it''s a damn shame that the horses didn''t want to cooperate and pose professionally for and that you can''t jump over fences and move other people''s horses.  I think this is a beautiful photo and you should be very proud of how it came out given the circumstances.', '2003-07-08 06:10:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(350131, 25801, 4162, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on getting a ribbon,  very well deserved! Was very happy to see this on the front page today.</td>', 'Congratulations on getting a ribbon,  very well deserved! Was very happy to see this on the front page today.', '2003-07-08 12:17:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(350198, 25801, 4090, '<td valign="top">Well done on yet another ribbon... it''s becoming a bit of a habit. keep them coming.</td>', 'Well done on yet another ribbon... it''s becoming a bit of a habit. keep them coming.', '2003-07-08 15:27:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(350354, 25650, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>Hey Jason! Fancy meeting you here!<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>I like parts of the composition here.  The symmetry is wonderful (good job using what we learned from a previous challenge).  However, I think the hay is the main interesting thing to look at and only takes up 25% of the surface of the picture.  We have a great zoom, and I would have used it to isolate the hay and the land around it.<br/> <br/><b>Color</b> <br/>Because you''re using browns, greens and blues, nothing pops here.  There is no ''wow'' color in this shot, like a guy in a yellow rain coat walking through the fields.  That would be interesting enough to take a picture of.  As it is, the subject must carry a greater weight and here the colors don''t contrast enough to set it off.<br/> <br/><b>Focus</b> <br/>Your focus is good - glad you didn''t get caught in the f2.8 to get enough light, focus on one bale of hay and there goes the focus issue.  I like landscapes being landscapey.  Our landscape mode works wonders - did you use that?<br/> <br/><b>Lighting</b> <br/>See color - with the white sky and no dramatic lighting, it is kind of flat and dull.  (no offense meant)  If the sun peeked through the clouds here 3 minutes later, again, you''d have something well worth taking a shot of.<br/> <br/><b>Subject matter</b> <br/>Hay = country.  You definitely met the challenge here.  I am as interested in hay as anything else I didn''t shoot myself, so good job here.<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/>I think the score was lower than you may have wanted because a) the light is dull and b) the colors are muted and almost appear washed out, plus the white sky phenomenon...<br/> <br/>You know I follow your work and you know I like it.  Good luck as always and keep shooting!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Hey Jason! Fancy meeting you here! Composition I like parts of the composition here.  The symmetry is wonderful (good job using what we learned from a previous challenge).  However, I think the hay is the main interesting thing to look at and only takes up 25% of the surface of the picture.  We have a great zoom, and I would have used it to isolate the hay and the land around it. Color Because you''re using browns, greens and blues, nothing pops here.  There is no ''wow'' color in this shot, like a guy in a yellow rain coat walking through the fields.  That would be interesting enough to take a picture of.  As it is, the subject must carry a greater weight and here the colors don''t contrast enough to set it off. Focus Your focus is good - glad you didn''t get caught in the f2.8 to get enough light, focus on one bale of hay and there goes the focus issue.  I like landscapes being landscapey.  Our landscape mode works wonders - did you use that? Lighting See color - with the white sky and no dramatic lighting, it is kind of flat and dull.  (no offense meant)  If the sun peeked through the clouds here 3 minutes later, again, you''d have something well worth taking a shot of. Subject matter Hay = country.  You definitely met the challenge here.  I am as interested in hay as anything else I didn''t shoot myself, so good job here. Overall I think the score was lower than you may have wanted because a) the light is dull and b) the colors are muted and almost appear washed out, plus the white sky phenomenon... You know I follow your work and you know I like it.  Good luck as always and keep shooting! ', '2003-07-08 19:03:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(350357, 25801, 6870, '<td valign="top">Wow, I didn''t realize this was a ribbon winner ''til yesterday. I''ll have to check the forums about what happened. Thanks for the votes; I sure admire a lot of your shots, too. Keep up the good work!</td>', 'Wow, I didn''t realize this was a ribbon winner ''til yesterday. I''ll have to check the forums about what happened. Thanks for the votes; I sure admire a lot of your shots, too. Keep up the good work!', '2003-07-08 19:05:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(350364, 26086, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>Hi Jim, nice to meet you!<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>This actually reminds me of one of my favorite shots on DPC: <a href="//www.dpcprints.com/print.php?IMAGE_ID=18771" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpcprints.com/print.php?IMAGE_ID=18771</a>  I hope you don''t mind me using it to make my comparisons.<br/> <br/>In your composition, you used a portrait view, to get more of the trees and a longer look at the path.  I think you lost the "isolated and lost" feeling from being in the woods with the inclusion of the sky.<br/> <br/><b>Color</b> <br/>The post processing done on crab''s shot to make the colors stand out is outstanding.  I would aspire to this kind of shot and these kinds of colors.  Your colors are much more yellowy, which may be realistic, but isn''t as pretty.<br/> <br/><b>Focus</b> <br/>Your focus is excellent.  I wonder what this would have looked like with an f2.0 or f2.8 though.  Focus near and have the further into the woods be less clear, as if your path is unclear...etc.  Just an idea.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting</b> <br/>The lighting here is a bit direct and harsh.  Looks more like a 2pm shot than a 5:30pm shot.  The lighting times DO make a difference and I''d aim to shoot in those golden hours (sunrise til 2 hrs later and sunset and 2 hrs before) as much as possible.<br/> <br/><b>Subject matter</b> <br/>Very interesting spot in nature.  I think this is a fine subject for a shot.<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/>I think the colors and lighting hurt your score.  Your cam is easily capable of matching the very best work on this site, so I hope you keep at it.<br/> <br/>Nice job on a first entry - way better than mine!<br/> <br/>Mav<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Hi Jim, nice to meet you! Composition This actually reminds me of one of my favorite shots on DPC: //www.dpcprints.com/print.php?IMAGE_ID=18771  I hope you don''t mind me using it to make my comparisons. In your composition, you used a portrait view, to get more of the trees and a longer look at the path.  I think you lost the "isolated and lost" feeling from being in the woods with the inclusion of the sky. Color The post processing done on crab''s shot to make the colors stand out is outstanding.  I would aspire to this kind of shot and these kinds of colors.  Your colors are much more yellowy, which may be realistic, but isn''t as pretty. Focus Your focus is excellent.  I wonder what this would have looked like with an f2.0 or f2.8 though.  Focus near and have the further into the woods be less clear, as if your path is unclear...etc.  Just an idea. Lighting The lighting here is a bit direct and harsh.  Looks more like a 2pm shot than a 5:30pm shot.  The lighting times DO make a difference and I''d aim to shoot in those golden hours (sunrise til 2 hrs later and sunset and 2 hrs before) as much as possible. Subject matter Very interesting spot in nature.  I think this is a fine subject for a shot. Overall I think the colors and lighting hurt your score.  Your cam is easily capable of matching the very best work on this site, so I hope you keep at it. Nice job on a first entry - way better than mine! Mav ', '2003-07-08 19:13:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(350378, 25360, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>Hi Steve, nice to meet you!<br/> <br/>I think your commenters all make sense.  Perhaps it is an English difficulty, so I will explain:<br/> <br/>Each week there is a challenge, or an idea.  If you wish to submit, take a picture with that idea.  The idea for this challenge was "country life" which means "living out in the country" or in a rural area.  For the photograph due tonight, that means "A night on the town" - what you do after dark with friends to spend time or something.<br/> <br/>If you do meet the challenge, you then can focus on lighting, color, focus, and trying to create a good picture.  You have a very nice camera, keep trying!<br/> <br/>Mav</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Hi Steve, nice to meet you! I think your commenters all make sense.  Perhaps it is an English difficulty, so I will explain: Each week there is a challenge, or an idea.  If you wish to submit, take a picture with that idea.  The idea for this challenge was "country life" which means "living out in the country" or in a rural area.  For the photograph due tonight, that means "A night on the town" - what you do after dark with friends to spend time or something. If you do meet the challenge, you then can focus on lighting, color, focus, and trying to create a good picture.  You have a very nice camera, keep trying! Mav', '2003-07-08 19:18:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(350387, 25932, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>Hi Mark!  First let me say I can''t believe how few comments you got on this.  I''m glad I picked it to CC.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>I think the perspective on the wheelbarrow is more of a journalistic "recording" of this than an interesting piece of art.  To me, it''s just a wheelbarrow.  While that could be said about a great many pictures, I think it really does apply here.  What made you want to take this photo like this?<br/> <br/><b>Color</b> <br/>Your colors are actually very weird.  I''m not sure why the logs look magenta, but I have a feeling it''s the same reason your grass looks unnaturally green.  I know we all like oversatured and supersaturated colors in some respects, but I think there''s a limit to what looks good (as I learned the hard way in Magazine challenge).<br/> <br/><b>Focus</b> <br/>Your focus is good here.  It doesn''t stand out, which is usually a good thing.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting</b> <br/>Flat lighting - not very interesting and it doesn''t add to the shot at all.<br/> <br/><b>Subject matter</b> <br/>As I said, it''s just a wheelbarrow with some grass.  I''m not sure if this is country life or suburb life, but it''s green, it''s living, so I''d say it meets the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/> <br/>I think Jason said it best "bit dull and lack of pop."  To do well on dpc, you need "WOW" factor, something interesting that would make someone want to come back and see it again.<br/> <br/>Keep shooting!<br/>Mav</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Hi Mark!  First let me say I can''t believe how few comments you got on this.  I''m glad I picked it to CC. Composition I think the perspective on the wheelbarrow is more of a journalistic "recording" of this than an interesting piece of art.  To me, it''s just a wheelbarrow.  While that could be said about a great many pictures, I think it really does apply here.  What made you want to take this photo like this? Color Your colors are actually very weird.  I''m not sure why the logs look magenta, but I have a feeling it''s the same reason your grass looks unnaturally green.  I know we all like oversatured and supersaturated colors in some respects, but I think there''s a limit to what looks good (as I learned the hard way in Magazine challenge). Focus Your focus is good here.  It doesn''t stand out, which is usually a good thing. Lighting Flat lighting - not very interesting and it doesn''t add to the shot at all. Subject matter As I said, it''s just a wheelbarrow with some grass.  I''m not sure if this is country life or suburb life, but it''s green, it''s living, so I''d say it meets the challenge. Overall  I think Jason said it best "bit dull and lack of pop."  To do well on dpc, you need "WOW" factor, something interesting that would make someone want to come back and see it again. Keep shooting!Mav', '2003-07-08 19:25:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(350393, 26308, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>Hi again, Stephan!  Always one of my fave photographers to comment on!<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>I think that you nailed it in your own comments - it could have used a main center of interest to tie the photo all together.  It could have been one of a lot of things, but kid with kite would have worked better.<br/> <br/><b>Color</b> <br/>I adore your sky - the shift in blues is amazing.  The higher in the sky, the better the colors.  Unfortunately, the ground is one big silhouette.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting</b> <br/>The lighting sets the sky like this, which is the best part of the picture, IMHO.<br/> <br/><b>Subject matter</b> <br/>Needs one.<br/> <br/><b>Overall</b> <br/>I think you know DPC well enough to understand what you said in your comments was your ultimate downfall.  We see the air around us, and it meets the challenge, but it just doesn''t have that "oooooh" or "wow" to it.<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Hi again, Stephan!  Always one of my fave photographers to comment on! Composition I think that you nailed it in your own comments - it could have used a main center of interest to tie the photo all together.  It could have been one of a lot of things, but kid with kite would have worked better. Color I adore your sky - the shift in blues is amazing.  The higher in the sky, the better the colors.  Unfortunately, the ground is one big silhouette. Lighting The lighting sets the sky like this, which is the best part of the picture, IMHO. Subject matter Needs one. Overall I think you know DPC well enough to understand what you said in your comments was your ultimate downfall.  We see the air around us, and it meets the challenge, but it just doesn''t have that "oooooh" or "wow" to it.  ', '2003-07-08 19:32:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(350521, 25651, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>The first thing I noticed about this shot was the beautiful deep, rich colors you have captured.  It appears to be early morning, or dusk, but that could just be the "darkness," of the photo.  I think if you could have done just one of a couple of things and made it a little lighter, so that more of the details would have "shown" through.  a)  bump ISO to 100.  May add a touch of grain, but I don''t know that it would have been noticeable.  b)  One or two stops lower on the aperture  c)  a notch or two slower on the shutter speed. <br/> <br/>The composition is good in that the chute helps to "anchor" the shot and give the picture a feeling of stability without seeming stagnant.  I do find myself wanting to see over it and into the horizon more.  I don''t know if it would have been possible to have a higher perspective, but that may have added to the shot, if you could have. <br/> <br/>Good work, and I hope to see more in future challenges.  If you have questions, comments, etc. please feel free to PM me.  Best to you.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat The first thing I noticed about this shot was the beautiful deep, rich colors you have captured.  It appears to be early morning, or dusk, but that could just be the "darkness," of the photo.  I think if you could have done just one of a couple of things and made it a little lighter, so that more of the details would have "shown" through.  a)  bump ISO to 100.  May add a touch of grain, but I don''t know that it would have been noticeable.  b)  One or two stops lower on the aperture  c)  a notch or two slower on the shutter speed.  The composition is good in that the chute helps to "anchor" the shot and give the picture a feeling of stability without seeming stagnant.  I do find myself wanting to see over it and into the horizon more.  I don''t know if it would have been possible to have a higher perspective, but that may have added to the shot, if you could have.  Good work, and I hope to see more in future challenges.  If you have questions, comments, etc. please feel free to PM me.  Best to you. karmat', '2003-07-08 23:49:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(350529, 26279, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>My first impression was "Cool subject, nasty colors."  ;-)<br/> <br/>I really like the composition of this with the silos falling on the right third of the shot.  The provide a nice vertical contrast to the horizontal "motion" from the barn.  I also like that the whole barn isn''t showing, though I''m not sure why.  The only suggestion I would make is to have more space in the foreground.  If there is a path or anything going to the barn, include it to give the eye something to follow.  (If there is not, get out and make one!!  [just kidding]).<br/> <br/>''Bout this coloring.  The "sepia" is a good idea.  I just think that it is perhaps too much yellow.  It looks jaundiced.  Maybe a bit more red to give it a more orangie look, or back way down on the saturation so that it is more bw with a hint of yellow.  That is, of course, just my opinion, but the yellow is so bold, it almost detracts from the subject of the picture -- which fits this challenge nicely!<br/> <br/>Best to you in the future.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat My first impression was "Cool subject, nasty colors."  ;-) I really like the composition of this with the silos falling on the right third of the shot.  The provide a nice vertical contrast to the horizontal "motion" from the barn.  I also like that the whole barn isn''t showing, though I''m not sure why.  The only suggestion I would make is to have more space in the foreground.  If there is a path or anything going to the barn, include it to give the eye something to follow.  (If there is not, get out and make one!!  [just kidding]). ''Bout this coloring.  The "sepia" is a good idea.  I just think that it is perhaps too much yellow.  It looks jaundiced.  Maybe a bit more red to give it a more orangie look, or back way down on the saturation so that it is more bw with a hint of yellow.  That is, of course, just my opinion, but the yellow is so bold, it almost detracts from the subject of the picture -- which fits this challenge nicely! Best to you in the future. karmat', '2003-07-08 23:58:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(350535, 26256, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>Nice shot for Country Life.  The horse looks so relaxed (though I seldom see horses looking any other way), and I think the simplicity of the shot works very well.<br/> <br/>I like the vertical crop on this, as I think the green "negative space" at the top does an effective job of directing attention to the  horse.  It might be a bit tight on the left and right, but it is balanced and doesn''t leave the viewer feeling like something was left out.<br/> <br/>The colors are very nice.  I like the contrast with the coloring of the horse, and the green grass.  The focus is a bit soft, can you adjust it in camera?  If not, perhaps a slightly larger aperture value or slower shutter speed would help (provided the horse doesn''t move!)<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges, and if you have questions or comments, please feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat Nice shot for Country Life.  The horse looks so relaxed (though I seldom see horses looking any other way), and I think the simplicity of the shot works very well. I like the vertical crop on this, as I think the green "negative space" at the top does an effective job of directing attention to the  horse.  It might be a bit tight on the left and right, but it is balanced and doesn''t leave the viewer feeling like something was left out. The colors are very nice.  I like the contrast with the coloring of the horse, and the green grass.  The focus is a bit soft, can you adjust it in camera?  If not, perhaps a slightly larger aperture value or slower shutter speed would help (provided the horse doesn''t move!) Best to you in future challenges, and if you have questions or comments, please feel free to PM me. karmat', '2003-07-09 00:06:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(350683, 26279, 6900, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the CC - I was trying to "yellow" it like an old photo starting to yellow from age.<br/> <br/>I thought I could shoehorn (LOL) it in with the title, but I guess not.</td>', 'Thanks for the CC - I was trying to "yellow" it like an old photo starting to yellow from age. I thought I could shoehorn (LOL) it in with the title, but I guess not.', '2003-07-09 02:05:09.000000', NULL, 0),
